# 11. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

Security is mandatory.

Implement server-side authorization for all privileged operations.

Apply least-privilege access.

Ordinary authenticated users must not be able to:

- Publish games directly.
- Approve submissions.
- Reject submissions.
- Send administrator review messages.
- Change administrator privileges.
- Read other users' private submission records.
- Read all users' private emails.
- Read pending APK files belonging to other users.
- Change a submission status to Approved.
- Change the publisher ID to an administrator ID.
- Write directly to privileged game records.

An ordinary user may create a submission only for their own authenticated account.

Only approved game metadata may be publicly readable without administrator authorization, subject to the application's intended privacy policy.

Private APK storage must not be publicly listable or readable without authorization.

Protect privileged APIs from unauthorized direct requests, even when called outside the website interface.

Use the identity provider's verified authentication context.

Keep secret configuration on the server.

Protect against basic abuse, unauthorized requests, forged metadata, duplicate submissions, and excessive login attempts.

Use secure production hosting with HTTPS.

Validate every important value on the backend.

Do not expose API secrets, service-account credentials, private storage configuration, or server environment variables to the browser.

Use a trusted scanning integration when available for uploaded APK files. Clearly distinguish between file-format validation and actual malware detection.

Never claim a file is malware-free just because it passed extension or archive validation.

# 12. DOWNLOAD AND STORAGE RELIABILITY

Implement reliable file storage and download management.

Store files under unique, non-guessable object paths.

Do not store APK binary content directly inside ordinary database documents.

Database records should store metadata and private storage paths.

Implement upload progress, appropriate timeouts, retry behavior, and understandable errors.

Handle interrupted uploads safely.

Ensure that a database record cannot remain published if the corresponding APK file was never successfully stored.

When an administrator removes a published game, follow a safe process that updates the publication status before deleting the file.

Before deleting a file, determine whether it is still referenced by another game record or requires retention for review history.

Do not immediately delete review evidence needed for an existing submission.

Implement appropriate cleanup for abandoned partial uploads where supported.

Do not silently report success if a database write or storage operation fails.

